.TH DATE 1
.SH NAME
date \- show the current date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
[-u]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print local date, time, timezone abbreviation and year. With -u, print UTC.
The kernel clock and file timestamps remain UTC regardless of display timezone.
.SH TIMEZONE
The TZ environment variable overrides the first line of /etc/TZ. An unset TZ
uses that system-wide file; an empty or invalid TZ selects UTC. Without either
setting, UTC is used. New libc builds support these settings on both FUZIX/Ripple
and Fuse-X; older statically linked programs need rebuilding to use them.
.PP
Use a POSIX-style rule: STDoffset, optionally followed by
DST[offset],start,end. Offsets are hours west of UTC, with optional minutes
and seconds; thus CET-1 is one hour east of UTC and EST5 is five hours west.
Names have 3 through 31 letters, or are enclosed in angle brackets to allow
digits and plus/minus signs. UTC and GMT alone are also accepted.
.PP
Daylight-saving zones require explicit start and end rules. Mm.w.d means
weekday d (Sunday is 0) in week w of month m; week 5 means the last occurrence.
Jn means Julian day 1 through 365 ignoring February 29. A bare n means day
0 through 365 counting leap days. Each rule may end in /hour[:minute[:second]];
the default is 02:00 local time before the transition. The summer offset
defaults to one hour ahead of standard time.
.PP
For Albania's current Central European rules:
.nf
TZ='CET-1CEST,M3.5.0/2,M10.5.0/3'
export TZ
.fi
.PP
For a persistent default used by shells, mux and newly built applications:
.nf
echo 'CET-1CEST,M3.5.0/2,M10.5.0/3' > /etc/TZ
.fi
.PP
An exported TZ takes precedence over that file; leave it unset when using the
system default. The rules repeat annually: IANA names such as Europe/Tirane,
binary zoneinfo files and historical rule changes are not implemented.
Changes to the host Mac's timezone do not change guest settings automatically.
.SH FILES
/etc/TZ
.SH SEE ALSO
mux(1), sh(1)
